00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
well we're joined now by stuart mckenzie  he's a british citizen who lives in kiev  and he has taken the decision to stay  there stuart thank you so much for  talking to us this afternoon how are you  feeling about everything that's going on  hi kimberly thank you very much um yeah  it's it's it's been a crazy 24 hours um  there's been a lot of uh thoughts and a  lot of the discussions and decisions  trying to be made  yesterday we were hoping that the biden  putin conversation  might create some sort of miracle it  didn't my wife and i took the decision  to to then pack up  we loaded the car we  booked to hotels we organized cash to  leave with her mother her mother-in-law  stays in the house  she's elderly she needs some care we  organized the people there  um  yeah put some jerry cans of extra fuel  in the car went to sleep  didn't sleep particularly well  and this morning you know just before we  were going to go  we couldn't do it we just couldn't leave  uh  where were you going to go stuart we're  going to go to poland i've got an office  in poland and we're going to go to the  border but we couldn't leave the  mother-in-law here my brother's also  here with two children they they didn't  leave i didn't want to leave them behind  we've got we've got thousands of friends  here we've got work colleagues  and and 95 of everybody's staying and uh  we we want to stand by them and and  we're not going to run away from the  bully in the schoolyard we're going to  stay together ukraine's done this before  of course with the orange revolutions  where neighbors  strangers friends all got together and  united they overcome these these these  challenges  so how has it affected you say it's been  it's been um a hectic  24 hours did you say 48 hours but it's  been building for weeks how has it  affected everyday life  everybody's trying to get on with their  lives we'll be at work as normal  tomorrow um like most people will be um  ukrainians are trying their best not to  saber rattle and and and hyped this up  they're trying to continue as peacefully  as possible to go about their normal  daily lives they're not past  you know buying  um  panic buying in the supermarkets or  anything like that it's a beautiful  sunny day here in cave and people are in  the parks and when walking the dogs is  normal um  but certainly when the embassies have  made their decision to leave  the multinational companies  you know they've taken their key staff  and left  um  it's it's just raised the stakes a  little bit um however  we're still talking of a country of 45  million people and big cities like i'm  in today kiev of you know almost 4  million people so together you know  that seems to be the  we don't really feel the immediate  threat of kiev or bombs coming down  uh if that does happen other people have  to make other decisions but is that up  to everybody individually you know some  people have medical issues some people  have young babies some people are  businesses  there's a multitude of decisions that  people have to make individually  how did you feel when the british  government said that british people  living there should get out i mean it  sounds easy doesn't it but you've lived  there for 28 years you can't just pack a  suitcase and get out can you well  exactly i mean imagine it was turned  around the other way and russia was  surrounding the uk just now and i'm the  ambassador and i'm telling you all to  leave  it's not easy right it's your home it's  your life it's everything and you know  why should we leave  right and why should we when the bully  comes in the schoolyard everybody runs  away  no what we do is we get more people like  us to the schoolyard  you know and you know so stop this um  there's easy wins for putin who could  try and take non-mass and things like  this we don't believe people here don't  believe that he's going to come in and  do a mass invasion of a country of 45  million people who don't want them here  um so  yes all support from the uk and other  markets are fantastic in terms of  armors that they can help defend the  country ukraine myself we don't need any  help for you know we don't expect  anybody coming from to our rescue but um  you know we're big boys we'll handle it  the way we can but we're certainly  not going to run away the first  first step  we've spoken to lots of people who um  civilians who've joined up or who say  that if needs be they will fight  have you do you have friends who say  that have you encountered people who say  if we have to we will defend our country  by any means possible  although definitely i know many of them  already that are doing that  um  you remember the maidan  revolutions and then  these guys were out in the street at  minus 20 in the in in hundreds of  thousands if not millions of people  sleeping overnight getting rid of you  know the president and and revolting  against decisions of rushing back  presidents  so um you know ukrainian people will  come together as they have done before  in extraordinary circumstances  and um but yes i definitely know people  that are signing up that are doing  anything possible we'll all do  things what we can do possible the  restaurants will give food the shops  will give clothes you know business  people will give money or services  whatever it's going to be  that's what they do in ukraine what's  the situation like in the shops can you  still get everything that you need  yeah yeah you can't get any bars iron  brew unfortunately but you can get  everything else yes sure it's not  there's no panic buying right now um  there's there's uh there's  everything is quite as normal you know  at the moment  this of course will be  if things escalate part of the problem  with distribution um and getting  products  distributed around the country banking  et cetera but let's again see where that  goes um you've got a whole country here  trying to make sure that the system will  continue to work and no matter what  aggression comes through  and how do people feel about zielinski  we saw him well he's continually  continually said we rely on our people  we rely on our army we saw those war  games just the other day do people  have faith in him do they trust him  yes i think you know i certainly support  what he's saying in terms of keep things  calm  he's the president on the ground of a  country under threat and i think that  the west should listen a little bit more  to the way he wants to deal with it  um rather than keep on  you know saber-rattling from the west  maybe um  he's the one that that he has to deal  with he's the one that's got the best  information on the ground information  and i think yes everybody will support  the president um because he's got the  best interests of the country at heart  so what happens now i'm assuming did you  unpack your car  not yet  it's still half packed we just don't  have the energy really it's it's it's  very emotional  no one can't even imagine what it's like  um  what happens if they do invade stuart  well we'll all make the decision at that  time um you know we've got whatsapp  groups we've got  you know uh  different  social media groups  um that everybody will communicate  through and and decisions will be made  at the time you know we've we've decided  at least that we're staying that's at  least something because when you're  indecisive it can cause a lot of stress  as well so when you made a decision and  then we're gonna we're gonna move from  that position and see how the days and  weeks go by  okay stuart um we understand you're in a  very difficult position to be in thank  you so much for talking to us this  afternoon thank you

